  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 218 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Period of dry weather continues for the north \ncountry as large ridge of surface high pressure nudges into our \narea out of Canada. Lots of dry air remains in place, and winds \nare out of the north following the passage of a weak surface \nboundary this morning. After afternoon ranging highs ranging \nthrough the 50s today, minimum temperatures overnight will dip \ninto the mid 20s to mid 30s, warmest along Lake Champlain where \nthe temperatures will be closer to 40. Ideal radiational cooling\nconditions, will also lead to some patchy fog developing in the\ntypically fog prone spots. Surface high will retreat to the \nnorth and east of our area tomorrow as a Nor'easter meanders off\nthe east coast. Wednesday night a weak cold front will cross \nour area, bringing colder conditions for Thursday and Friday to \nclose out the work week, with another surface ridge of high \npressure building into our area from the northwest. Maximum \ntemperatures on Friday will struggle to get out of the 40s. \nWinds will also be brisk out of the north, especially on \nThursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A classic stretch of fall weather will continue \nthis weekend and into the beginning of next week. Surface high \npressure will be centered almost directly overhead over the \nweekend, leading to clear skies, light winds and highs in the \n50s. Most mornings will be frosty with lows in the 20s and 30s. \nA moisture starved cold front moves through Monday and Monday \nnight and while a brief sprinkle or mountain flurry is possible \nduring its passage, it will be pretty much completely dry. \nTemperatures rise to around 60 Monday, before highs drop back \ninto the 50s and potentially upper 40s for Tuesday.",
